{"team":"LAC","col":"#3AC7F0","identity":"run-heavy","funnel":"balanced-D","plays_pg":60.9,"adot":8.4,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>Bleed the Clock</h2><p>There's no bell-cow to bank on \u2014 the lead back sees just <strong>47%</strong> of carries (league ~59%), a committee that caps any one back's ceiling and makes their rushing props matchup- and script-dependent.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>LAC throw by design. They pass <strong>-0%</strong> over expectation (league -2%) and keep firing even with a lead (40% early-down pass when ahead vs ~41%) \u2014 the kind of pass-first identity that keeps receivers live in every script.</p><p>When they fall behind they abandon the run entirely \u2014 63% pass while trailing, so their pass-catchers have huge comeback-script ceilings.</p><p>The WR2 is unusually involved here \u2014 <strong>19%</strong> of targets (league ~15%), meaning there's bankable volume beyond the top receiver.</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they lean run-heavy, passing <strong>50%</strong> of the time, funnelling touchdown equity to <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong>.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 A Balanced Defense</h2><p>They surrender <strong>115 rushing</strong> and <strong>212 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). No dominant funnel \u2014 read it matchup by matchup.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR2s \u2014 just 32 rec yds/game (vs ~36); fade receiving props there.</p><p>A stingy, efficient defense \u2014 just 42% success allowed (vs ~44%); temper expectations for opposing skill players.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: back <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong>'s rushing volume in favored/positive scripts; their pass-catchers have massive ceilings in comeback scripts (fade-material as underdogs).</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the current staff and roster set their own tendencies.</em></p></div>","depth":{"QB":[{"name":"Justin Herbert","slot":"QB1","